 ],
 "literal": "Does he not think [of us], friend? Even if he thinks, / because of the absence of a matching opportunity, does he not come? / Having struggled to eat bowstring hemp, the dark-necked stag / sleeps in the striped shade of the Yā tree left behind after being broken and eaten by the mortar-legged elephant, / in the mountains of the dark vast grove that he traversed.",
 "translation": "Does he not think of us, my friend? Or, even if he does, is it because he has no opportunity to return that he does not come? He who traversed the mountains of the dark, vast forest, where a black-necked stag, having struggled to feed on tough bowstring hemp, now sleeps in the striped shade of a yā tree—broken, eaten, and left behind by an elephant with legs like mortars.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! அவர் நம்மை நினைக்கவில்லையா? நினைத்தாலும், திரும்பி வருவதற்கான வாய்ப்பு இல்லாததால் வரவில்லையா? உரல் போன்ற கால்களைக் கொண்ட யானை ஒடித்துத் தின்றுவிட்டு மீதி வைத்த யா மரத்தின் கோடுகளையுடைய நிழலில், மரல் நாரைத் தின்று வருந்திய கரிய கழுத்தையுடைய ஆண் மான் உறங்கும் கரிய பெரிய சோலைகளைக் கொண்ட மலையைக் கடந்து சென்றவர்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the friend to the heroine during the hero's separation in the wasteland (pālai) landscape. The friend consoles the heroine by suggesting that the hero's delay is not due to forgetfulness, but rather due to a lack of opportunity to return. To illustrate the harsh yet hopeful nature of the path he took, she describes a stag that struggles to feed on tough bowstring hemp. The stag eventually finds comfort and sleeps in the striped shade of a yā tree, which was broken and partially eaten by a massive elephant. This image of the elephant leaving behind a portion of the tree that now shelters the weary stag suggests that even in the most destructive and dry environments, relief and survival are possible, hinting that the hero will also find a way back to comfort the heroine.",
